## JPA & Hibernate Performance Architecture

### The N+1 Query Disaster

```java
// Step 1: 1 SQL query to load 100 departments
List<Department> depts = departmentRepository.findAll();

// Step 2: N (100) individual SQL queries triggered on lazy collection access!
for (Department dept : depts) {
    System.out.println(dept.getEmployees().size()); // 100 queries!
}
// Total = 1 + 100 = 101 queries executed!
```

### The 4 Production Solutions to N+1

#### Solution 1: `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL (Most Common)
```java
@Query("SELECT DISTINCT d FROM Department d LEFT JOIN FETCH d.employees")
List<Department> findAllWithEmployees();
// ✅ Exactly 1 query executed with SQL JOIN
```

#### Solution 2: JPA `@EntityGraph` (Declarative)
```java
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"employees", "employees.badges"})
List<Department> findByRegion(String region);
```

#### Solution 3: Hibernate Batch Size (`@BatchSize`)
```java
@Entity
public class Department {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department")
    @BatchSize(size = 30) // Fetches 30 collections in a single 'WHERE dept_id IN (?, ?, ...)'
    private List<Employee> employees;
}
```

#### Solution 4: DTO Constructor Projections (Best for Read APIs)
```java
@Query("SELECT new com.app.dto.DeptSummaryDTO(d.id, d.name, COUNT(e.id)) " +
       "FROM Department d LEFT JOIN d.employees e GROUP BY d.id, d.name")
List<DeptSummaryDTO> getSummaries();
```

### Locking: Optimistic vs Pessimistic

| Lock Type | Mechanism | SQL Generated | Ideal Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Optimistic** | `@Version` column (int or timestamp) | `UPDATE ... WHERE id = ? AND version = ?` | High read, low contention (e.g. user profile updates) |
| **Pessimistic** | Database row-level locks | `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` | High contention financial transfers, ticket booking |

## Pessimistic vs Optimistic Locking in JPA

### The Problem: Lost Updates
Without locking, two concurrent transactions can both read the same value, each modify it, and both write back — the second write silently overwrites the first.

```
T1: READ balance=100 → compute 100+50=150 → WRITE 150
T2: READ balance=100 → compute 100+30=130 → WRITE 130  ← Lost T1's +50!
```

### Optimistic Locking with @Version
Best for **low contention** scenarios (most reads, occasional writes). No database locks held — conflict detected at commit time.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;
    private int stockQuantity;

    @Version  // Hibernate manages this automatically
    private int version;  // or Long, or Timestamp
}
```

Hibernate generates:
```sql
-- UPDATE with version check:
UPDATE products
SET stock_quantity = ?, version = 2
WHERE id = 1 AND version = 1;  -- Fails if another transaction already incremented version!
-- If 0 rows updated → throws OptimisticLockException (wraps StaleObjectStateException)
```

**Retry logic for optimistic locking:**
```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Retryable(
        value = OptimisticLockingFailureException.class,
        maxAttempts = 3,
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 100, multiplier = 2)
    )
    @Transactional
    public void reserveStock(Long productId, int qty) {
        Product product = productRepository.findById(productId).orElseThrow();
        if (product.getStockQuantity() < qty) throw new InsufficientStockException();
        product.setStockQuantity(product.getStockQuantity() - qty);
        productRepository.save(product);
    }
}
```

### Pessimistic Locking with @Lock
Best for **high contention** scenarios where conflicts are frequent and retry cost is high (financial transactions, seat booking).

```java
public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, Long> {
    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)  // → SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.id = :id")
    Optional<Account> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);

    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_READ)   // → SELECT ... FOR SHARE
    Optional<Account> findById(Long id);
}
```

Generated SQL:
```sql
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE; -- Blocks other writers!
-- Other transactions wanting FOR UPDATE must WAIT until this transaction commits
```

### Lock Mode Types Comparison

| LockModeType | SQL | Behavior |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `OPTIMISTIC` | (none at SELECT) | Version check at commit |
| `OPTIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT` | (none at SELECT) | Always increments version |
| `PESSIMISTIC_READ` | `SELECT FOR SHARE` | Blocks writers, allows other readers |
| `PESSIMISTIC_WRITE` | `SELECT FOR UPDATE` | Blocks all other readers & writers |
| `PESSIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT` | `SELECT FOR UPDATE` | Blocks + increments version |

### Decision Guide

| Scenario | Strategy | Reason |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| User profile update | Optimistic | Rarely concurrent, retry is cheap |
| Bank transfer | Pessimistic | High contention, lost update is catastrophic |
| Flash sale inventory | Pessimistic + queue | Extreme contention |
| Document versioning | Optimistic | Show user a 'conflict' dialog |

### Deadlock Prevention with Pessimistic Locking
Always acquire locks **in a consistent order** across all transactions to prevent deadlocks:
```java
// Always lock lower ID first:
Long firstId = Math.min(fromId, toId);
Long secondId = Math.max(fromId, toId);
Account first  = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(firstId);
Account second = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(secondId);
```

## MongoDB Document Modeling

### The Core Design Decision: Embed vs Reference

MongoDB's schema design revolves around one question: **should related data live inside the same document or be referenced across collections?**

#### Rule of Thumb

| Relationship | Recommendation | Reason |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| One-to-few (1:few) | **Embed** | Single read, data rarely changes |
| One-to-many (1:many) | **Reference** | Avoid large document bloat |
| Many-to-many (M:N) | **Reference** | Junction document or arrays of IDs |
| Frequently read together | **Embed** | Avoid application-side joins |
| Independently updated | **Reference** | Avoid update contention |

```javascript
// ✅ Embed: Blog post with few comments (1:few)
{
  _id: ObjectId("..."),
  title: "MongoDB Modeling",
  author: "Abhinav",
  comments: [
    { user: "Alice", text: "Great post!", date: ISODate("2026-10-22") },
    { user: "Bob",   text: "Very helpful", date: ISODate("2026-10-22") }
  ]
}

// ✅ Reference: Order referencing Product (1:many, independent updates)
// orders collection:
{ _id: ObjectId("ord1"), customerId: ObjectId("cust1"),
  items: [ { productId: ObjectId("prod1"), qty: 2, price: 29.99 } ] }

// products collection:
{ _id: ObjectId("prod1"), name: "Laptop", stock: 50 }
```

### Aggregation Pipeline
The aggregation pipeline transforms documents through stages:

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  // Stage 1: Filter
  { $match: { status: "COMPLETED", createdAt: { $gte: ISODate("2026-01-01") } } },

  // Stage 2: Join products collection
  { $lookup: {
    from: "products",
    localField: "items.productId",
    foreignField: "_id",
    as: "productDetails"
  }},

  // Stage 3: Flatten arrays
  { $unwind: "$items" },

  // Stage 4: Group and aggregate
  { $group: {
    _id: "$customerId",
    totalSpent:  { $sum: { $multiply: ["$items.price", "$items.qty"] } },
    orderCount:  { $sum: 1 }
  }},

  // Stage 5: Reshape output
  { $project: {
    customerId: "$_id",
    totalSpent: 1,
    orderCount: 1,
    avgOrderValue: { $divide: ["$totalSpent", "$orderCount"] },
    _id: 0
  }},

  // Stage 6: Sort
  { $sort: { totalSpent: -1 } },
  { $limit: 10 }
]);
```

### MongoDB Index Types

```javascript
// Single field index
db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 }); // 1=ascending, -1=descending

// Compound index (leftmost prefix rule applies same as SQL!)
db.orders.createIndex({ customerId: 1, createdAt: -1 });

// Text index for full-text search
db.products.createIndex({ name: "text", description: "text" });
db.products.find({ $text: { $search: "wireless keyboard" } });

// Geospatial index
db.stores.createIndex({ location: "2dsphere" });
db.stores.find({ location: { $near: { $geometry: { type: "Point",
  coordinates: [-122.4, 37.7] }, $maxDistance: 5000 } } });

// Partial index (index subset of documents)
db.orders.createIndex(
  { customerId: 1, createdAt: -1 },
  { partialFilterExpression: { status: "PENDING" } }
);
```

### ACID in MongoDB 4+
MongoDB 4.0+ supports multi-document ACID transactions across a replica set:

```javascript
const session = client.startSession();
try {
  session.startTransaction();
  await db.accounts.updateOne(
    { _id: fromId }, { $inc: { balance: -amount } }, { session }
  );
  await db.accounts.updateOne(
    { _id: toId }, { $inc: { balance: +amount } }, { session }
  );
  await session.commitTransaction();
} catch (e) {
  await session.abortTransaction();
} finally {
  session.endSession();
}
```
